that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nSubstituting our data in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546969 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=-9,$ $\\text{SD}[X]=2,$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]=5$, find the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a6faf2d-d933-5235-81aa-8381b3e3dfb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546969 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nSubstituting our data in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546972 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546972 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=15,$ $\\text{SD}[X]=10,$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]=5$, find the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ca653e0-5f41-50c6-a86a-21726824fff4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546972 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nSubstituting our data in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{10\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546977 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{9},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{9},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.5329,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2833,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2265fb13-ede5-5876-96c9-a4518515ab31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546977 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{x+y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-(\\frac{11}{9})(\\frac{5}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{(0.5329)\\cdot (0.2833)} \\\\  & ≈-0.08,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546989 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2898,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2764,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37a89027-b700-5936-918f-1cda621eae37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546989 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{1}{2}xy)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{3x}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3x}+\\frac{1}{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}+\\frac{3}{8})-(\\frac{3}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}-\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12})+(\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{7}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12}-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12}-\\frac{1}{16})}{(0.2898)\\cdot (0.2764)} \\\\  & ≈-0.06,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547013 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547013 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547013 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547013 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.4332,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.4332,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547013 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6da1a8e2-af30-5f0e-8a87-d537d126dcee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547013 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician and integration by parts, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}xy\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\frac{\\pi }{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-y)]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi +8}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi +8}{8}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-8\\pi +16}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1)}^{2}}{(0.4332)\\cdot (0.4332)} \\\\  & ≈0.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547017 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547017 #edb/ref 1071 "The variances of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ are $0.09$ and $0.16$, respectively. Given that $\\text{Cov}(X,Y)=-0.03,$ find the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "725ce851-768b-5450-a6f8-19b77ae7d6ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547017 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{0.09}=0.3, \\\\ \\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[Y]}=\\sqrt{0.16}=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our data in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[X]} \\\\  & =\\frac{-0.03}{0.3\\cdot 0.4} \\\\  & =-0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547020 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=-10$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=10$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.4$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.45$ | $0.1$ | $0.45$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=2,\\,\\text{E}[Y]=0,\\,\\text{SD}[X]=2,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈9.4868,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77a83823-b66d-5cc9-b223-222b52ef5845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547020 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-10)\\cdot 0.4+0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.05+0\\cdot 10\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & +4\\cdot (-10)\\cdot 0.05+4\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.05+4\\cdot 10\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =14-(2)(0) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{14}{2\\cdot 9.4868} \\\\  & ≈0.74,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547024 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.8$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.8\\,\\text{E}[Y]=0.7,\\,\\text{SD}[X]=0.4,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.4583,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7da918be-db4c-59cf-8e95-bb2e34ce04e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547024 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & +1\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.2+1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.6-(0.8)(0.7) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.04}{0.4\\cdot 0.4583} \\\\  & ≈0.22,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547028 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{12}(xy+1), & \\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{7},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2619,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.4238,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86de9def-10fe-5788-a466-3607900726ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547028 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{9}{12}(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}y-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{8}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}{[\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{27}{y}^{9}-\\frac{1}{12}{y}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}[\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{27}-\\frac{1}{12}-(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{27}-\\frac{1}{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}(\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{2}{27}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}-(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{315}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{8}{315})}{(0.2619)\\cdot (0.4238)} \\\\  & ≈0.23,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547063 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{\\pi }, & \\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{8}{5\\pi },\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{8}{5\\pi },\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2719,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2719,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db6e411e-8a97-527f-83cb-17311240c8a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547063 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}xy\\cdot \\frac{8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }\\text{ }{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}({x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{4}{(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}(1-{y}^{2}){y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}[(1-2{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})y+2(1-{y}^{2}){y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }-(\\frac{8}{5\\pi })(\\frac{8}{5\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{50\\pi -192}{75{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{50\\pi -192}{75{\\pi }^{2}})}{(0.2719)\\cdot (0.2719)} \\\\  & ≈-0.64,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
